<h2 id="optional-accessing-user-data-elsewhere" class="anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-tighter"><span>Optional: Accessing user data elsewhere</span><a class="rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden" href="#optional-accessing-user-data-elsewhere" aria-label="Link to Optional: Accessing user data elsewhere"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="14" height="14" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 14 14" class="inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3.5"><path stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="square" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="1.4" d="m3.938 12.688 1.75-11.375M8.313 12.688l1.75-11.375M3.063 4.813h8.75M2.188 9.188h8.75"></path></svg></a></h2>
<p>While this guide focused on <strong>Server Actions</strong> to handle data, your application might need to access the user&#x27;s session in other contexts, such as rendering a user profile on the server, reacting to session changes on the client, or securing a REST API endpoint.</p>
